    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i tried using the hairdryer on the base like you said but the error code just came right back on after like 5 minutes of running. it's like the water is just getting back in there immediately. is there some specific spot in the bottom tray where the sensor is located? it's kind of hard to see down in there.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for my oversight. The leak sensor is indeed located at the bottom back of the dishwasher. It's usually near the bottom wall at the very back, on the side farthest away from the front. Make sure to unplug the dishwasher first, remove any plastic compartments, and gently rinse down the side wall where the sensor is. Tackle the base by scraping away the tight dishwasher seal and removing as much sticky grime or residue as possible so you can make sure whether it's clean or not. Continue running a short wash cycle with vinegar on a ceramic wash disk, then rinse again and run another cycle to see whether that causes the dishwasher to leak. If everything looks clean and there's only a small puddle or residue left, running a water test cycle would tell whether there's any leak. Remember to clean the sensor again and touch up any loose connections to make sure it's clean as well. Check if that solves the problem. However, if it's still inaccessible or hard to clean, you may need more advanced tools and tricks to solve the problem, especially given the potential 2010 model.",
