    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's indeed possible that something could be stuck in there that you're not seeing. Since I'm unable to physically see or touch anything, try the following:\n\n1. Remove the upper rack, sufficiently dry it, and wipe leaving less residue or grime on it.\n\n2. Put the upper rack back and carefully run the dishwasher on a short high-cycle to check if anything comes loose.\n\n3. After the cycle, check the top corner of the inside walls (opposite the dishwasher's front) for any sharp parts that somehow may weigh down the sensor from the sensors' weight range. If it feels loose, it'll likely fall out. You may be stuck in there with a dishwasher that needs a call to a technician.\n\nAs I can verify, it's very hard to reach the base of most dishwashers. Some parts can be dismantled completely in front of you, making it easier to reach, which I can't do if I could see or feel things. Hopefully, there's no damage to any components in that would make things worse. It's possible you'd need a sharp or long object (not a screwdriver or coin etc.), a heated wire brush or a power drill on the lowest setting, to get at the sensor. Note this is very, very hard, and purely",
